WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.745
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.045 --> 00:00:15.948
Unit eighteen: Advanced Comparatives. Today you will show gradual change and big differences.

4
00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.162
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:25.064
The more...the more.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.758
The more...the more means two things increase together.

8
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.477
The more I read, the more I know.

9
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:35.096
Your turn. Say: The more...the more.

10
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:39.039
The more...the more.

11
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:40.738
By far.

12
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.889
By far means by a very large amount.

13
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:45.775
By far the best.

14
00:00:46.383 --> 00:00:48.734
Your turn. Say: By far.

15
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.566
By far.

16
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:54.504
Increasingly.

17
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:57.597
Increasingly means more and more.

18
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:00.020
It is increasingly difficult.

19
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:03.385
Your turn. Say: Increasingly.

20
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:07.583
Increasingly.

21
00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:09.736
Progressive comparison.

22
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.712
Progressive comparison means a comparative repeated for gradual change.

23
00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:16.620
Cheaper and cheaper.

24
00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:20.519
Your turn. Say: Progressive comparison.

25
00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:24.832
Progressive comparison.

26
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:26.956
Double comparative.

27
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.646
Double comparative means the comparative repeated.

28
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.553
Bigger and bigger.

29
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.325
Your turn. Say: Double comparative.

30
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.748
Double comparative.

31
00:01:41.483 --> 00:01:42.426
Far more.

32
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.590
Far more means a much larger amount.

33
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:47.602
Far more expensive.

34
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:50.532
Your turn. Say: Far more.

35
00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:54.333
Far more.

36
00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:56.526
A great deal more.

37
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.656
A great deal more means a lot more.

38
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.797
A great deal more money.

39
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:05.349
Your turn. Say: A great deal more.

40
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:09.549
A great deal more.

41
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:11.714
Nuanced comparison.

42
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:16.005
Nuanced comparison means a careful, subtle comparison.

43
00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:18.210
A nuanced comparison.

44
00:02:18.817 --> 00:02:21.829
Your turn. Say: Nuanced comparison.

45
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.179
Nuanced comparison.

46
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:30.943
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:31.783 --> 00:02:34.909
Visal, complete: the more I study, the…

48
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:44.256
The more I study, the better I understand. This double comparative shows that two changes happen together. Both parts begin with the.

49
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.326
How do we show gradual change?

50
00:02:48.417 --> 00:03:03.729
We repeat the comparative: The weather is getting colder and colder. Phones are becoming cheaper and cheaper. With long adjectives we use more and more: more and more expensive. Increasingly is also common.

51
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.990
How do we modify comparatives?

52
00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:22.557
By far with a superlative: by far the best. Far, much, a great deal and slightly with a comparative: far more difficult, slightly cheaper. These choices give a nuanced comparison.

53
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.328
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:31.283
Grammar focus: Advanced comparatives: the more… the more, by far, increasingly.

55
00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:32.804
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:35.795
The more I study, the better I understand.

57
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.054
Phones are becoming cheaper and cheaper.

58
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.961
This is by far the best option.

59
00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:52.036
The + comparative, the + comparative. Cheaper and cheaper = gradual change. By far + superlative. Far / much / a great deal / slightly + comparative.

60
00:03:52.783 --> 00:03:56.411
Be careful! Don't say: The more I study, more I learn.

61
00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:01.179
Say: The more I study, the more I learn.

62
00:04:01.479 --> 00:04:02.712
Use the twice.

63
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.222
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:08.151
More and More.

65
00:04:08.451 --> 00:04:10.833
The more I read, the more I understand.

66
00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:14.738
Life in cities is becoming more and more expensive.

67
00:04:15.733 --> 00:04:18.186
This is by far the best course I have taken.

68
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.279
The test was far more difficult than last year's test.

69
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:25.754
These comparisons are increasingly nuanced.

70
00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:31.008
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.942
The blank you practise, the better you get.

72
00:04:34.242 --> 00:04:37.501
A, more. B, most. C, much.

73
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:42.834
The answer is A: more.

74
00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.994
The weather is getting blank and blank.

75
00:04:46.294 --> 00:04:51.167
A, colder; colder. B, cold; cold. C, coldest; coldest.

76
00:04:54.733 --> 00:04:57.116
The answer is A: colder; colder.

77
00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.297
This is blank the best film. (by a large amount)

78
00:05:01.597 --> 00:05:05.552
A, far by. B, very far. C, by far.

79
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.007
The answer is C: by far.

80
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.975
Which means 'a small difference'?

81
00:05:14.275 --> 00:05:19.113
A, far cheaper. B, slightly cheaper. C, much cheaper.

82
00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:25.409
The answer is B: slightly cheaper.

83
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.850
According to the reading, what is becoming more expensive?

84
00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:34.082
A, life in cities. B, food. C, bread.

85
00:05:37.633 --> 00:05:40.155
The answer is A: life in cities.

86
00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:44.240
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:47.733
Write a sentence using 'the more...the more' structure.

88
00:05:56.517 --> 00:05:57.947
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:58.247 --> 00:06:09.296
The more I practise English, the more confident I feel. The more confident I feel, the more I speak. The two parts show that two changes happen together.

90
00:06:09.596 --> 00:06:11.375
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:12.283 --> 00:06:18.976
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and advanced comparatives: the more… the more, by far, increasingly.

92
00:06:19.276 --> 00:06:24.880
Today's value is growth mindset. Mistakes help you grow. Keep learning, step by step.

93
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:27.832
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:28.132 --> 00:06:31.261
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

